🎯OOP Interview Preparation Story Mode
Interviewer: What is OOP?
You:

Imagine we are building a Food Delivery App.

In this app we have:

Customers
Restaurants
Delivery Partners
Orders

Each of them has some data and some actions.

For example:

Customer

Data:

Name = Pooja
Phone = 9876543210

Actions:

Place Order
Cancel Order
Track Order

So instead of writing everything separately, we create objects.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This approach is called Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
👉 OOP = Organizing software around objects having data and behavior.

🎯 What is a Class?
Interviewer:

What is a class?

You:

A class is a blueprint.

Before building actual customers, we define:

class Customer {
    String name;
    String phone;

    void placeOrder(){}
}

This blueprint is called a class.

Real Life Example

Think of a cookie cutter.

Cookie Cutter = Class

Cookies = Objects

One cutter can create many cookies.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🎯 What is an Object?
Interviewer:

What is an object?

You:

An object is a real instance created from a class.

Customer c1 = new Customer();
Customer c2 = new Customer();

Now:

c1 = Pooja
c2 = Rahul

Both are objects.

Memory Visualization
Customer Class
      |
      |
 -------------
 |           |
c1          c2
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 What is Encapsulation?
Interviewer:

Explain encapsulation.

You:

Encapsulation means:

Hide data and allow controlled access.

Food Delivery Example

Customer wallet balance:

private int walletBalance;

Can we allow anyone to do:

walletBalance = -10000;

❌ No

That would be dangerous.

Instead:

addMoney()
deductMoney()
getBalance()
Real Life Example
ATM Machine

You see:

Withdraw
Deposit
Check Balance

You do NOT see:

database_balance_column

Hidden data = Encapsulation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🎯 What is Inheritance?
Interviewer:

Explain inheritance.

You:

Inheritance means reusing existing code.

Food Delivery Example

Every person in the app has:

Name
Phone
Address

Instead of writing repeatedly:

class Person{
    String name;
    String phone;
}

Then:

class Customer extends Person
class DeliveryBoy extends Person
class RestaurantOwner extends Person

All inherit common properties.

Real World
Animal
   |
  Dog

Dog automatically gets:

eat()
sleep()

from Animal.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 Why Inheritance?
Interviewer:

Why use inheritance?

You:

Without inheritance:

Customer
name
phone

DeliveryBoy
name
phone

RestaurantOwner
name
phone

Repeated code.

With inheritance:

Person

contains common code once.

Less code.
Easy maintenance.

